'''Ìbẹmìílò''' ({{Literal translation|the way of the earth}}" or "that which the earth-dwellers follow") is the traditional [[W:Ethnic religion|ethnic religion]] of the [[Ayori people]], originating in the [[Ayori city-states]] of present-day [[Ayorin]] and practiced predominantly in the southern half of the country. It is a [[W:Polytheism|polytheistic]] and [[W:Animism|animistic]] faith centered on the veneration of deities known as ''[[Ìmọ̀lẹ̀]]'' (often translated as "divinities" or "orishas"), [[W:Ancestor veneration|ancestor veneration]], and the practice of ''[[W:Ifá|Ifá]]'' divination.

With approximately 45 million adherents, Ìbẹmìílò is one of the largest traditional religions in [[Kaftia]] and serves as a foundational element of [[Ayori culture]]. The religion has significantly influenced the syncretic forms of [[Christianity in Ayorin|Christianity]] practiced in the northern regions of Ayorin, particularly the [[Zamarra Catholic Church]], which incorporates many Ìbẹmìílò beliefs, rituals, and symbols.

The name ''Ìbẹmìílò'' derives from the Imo phrase ''ìbẹ̀ mì ílò'', meaning "the practice of those who dwell on the earth." This reflects the religion's emphasis on the interconnectedness of all earthly beings—human, divine, and natural—and the importance of maintaining harmony between the material and spiritual realms.

==Theology and cosmology==
{{Main|Ìbẹmìílò cosmology}}

===Olódùmarè and the Supreme Deity===

Ìbẹmìílò theology posits a supreme creator deity, '''Olódùmarè''' (Imo: {{lang|yo|Olódùmarè}}), also known as '''Ẹlédáa''' ("The Creator") or '''Olorun''' ("Owner of the Sky"). Olódùmarè is considered the source of all existence, the ultimate reality from which all things emanate. Unlike the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' (lesser divinities), Olódùmarè is [[transcendence (religion)|transcendent]] and is not typically worshipped directly through shrines or sacrifices; rather, devotion is directed through the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' who serve as intermediaries.

The relationship between Olódùmarè and the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' is often described through the metaphor of a great king and his ministers: the king (Olódùmarè) rules the kingdom, but his ministers (''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'') manage specific domains and interact directly with the people. This theological structure allows for a highly developed pantheon while maintaining a fundamentally [[henotheistic]] or [[monolatrous]] worldview.

===The Ìmọ̀lẹ̀ (Divinities)===
{{Main|Ìmọ̀lẹ̀}}

The ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' (singular: ''Ìmọ̀lẹ́'') are divine beings who govern specific aspects of nature, human endeavor, and cosmic forces. They are considered emanations of Olódùmarè's divine energy (''àṣẹ'') and serve as the primary focus of worship, prayer, and sacrifice in Ìbẹmìílò practice. The number of ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' is vast—oral traditions speak of 401, 1,600, or even countless divinities—though a core pantheon of major deities is recognized across most Ayori communities.

