Each ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' has associated colors, numbers, foods, animals, and ritual practices. Devotees often develop a personal relationship with one or more ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' who serve as their spiritual guardians and advocates before Olódùmarè.

===Àṣẹ (Divine Power)===

Central to Ìbẹmìílò cosmology is the concept of '''àṣẹ''' (Imo: {{lang|yo|àṣẹ}}), often translated as "divine power," "life force," or "the power to make things happen." Àṣẹ is the fundamental energy that permeates all existence—the force through which Olódùmarè created the universe and through which the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' exercise their powers.

Human beings possess àṣẹ, which can be cultivated through proper living, ritual practice, and alignment with one's destiny (''ọ̀rọ́''). The spoken word is considered a particularly potent vehicle for àṣẹ; hence, prayers, incantations, and the naming of things carry creative and transformative power. The common Imo phrase ''"Àṣẹ!"'' (roughly "May it be so!" or "Amen!") is used to affirm and seal prayers and pronouncements.

===Destiny and the Ori===

Ìbẹmìílò teaches that each person possesses an '''Ori''' (literally "head"), an individual spiritual consciousness that contains one's destiny (''ọ̀rọ́'') chosen before birth. Before incarnating on earth, each soul selects its destiny at the "Palace of Choices" (''Ọ̀run Ọ̀kẹ̀''), determining the major events, challenges, and achievements of their earthly life.

However, the chosen destiny is not immutable; through proper alignment with one's Ori, ethical living, and ritual practice, individuals can fulfill their highest potential. Conversely, neglecting one's Ori or living contrary to one's destiny leads to misfortune and spiritual imbalance. The practice of Ifá divination is often employed to discover one's destiny and receive guidance on fulfilling it.

===Cosmology: Ayé and Ọ̀run===

The Ìbẹmìílò cosmos is divided into two primary realms:

* '''Ayé''' (the material world): The realm of human existence, the earth, and visible reality. This is the domain of daily life, where humans interact with each other, nature, and the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' through ritual.

* '''Ọ̀run''' (the spiritual realm): The realm of the ancestors, the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'', and Olódùmarè. It is the source of all being and the destination of souls after death. The boundary between Ayé and Ọ̀run is considered permeable; spirits can cross between realms, and humans can access Ọ̀run through dreams, trance, and ritual.

The two realms are connected by '''Ẹsù''', the divine messenger who traverses boundaries and facilitates communication between humans and the divine. This cosmological structure emphasizes the interconnection of all things and the possibility of direct relationship between the material and spiritual dimensions of existence.

==Practices and rituals==
{{Main|Ìbẹmìílò practices}}

===Ifá divination===
{{Main|Ifá}}

'''Ifá''' is a complex system of [[divination]] that forms the cornerstone of Ìbẹmìílò religious practice. It is performed by trained priests called '''Babalawo''' (Imo: {{lang|yo|Babaláwo}}, "Father of Secrets") or, in Ekwe-speaking regions, '''Dibia''' (Ekwe: {{lang|ig|Dibia}}). The diviner interprets patterns formed by casting divining chains (''ọpẹ̀lẹ̀'') or palm nuts (''ikín'') to reveal messages from Ọ̀rúnmìlà, the orisha of wisdom and destiny.

Ifá is consulted for virtually all major life decisions—marriage, career, health, travel, and spiritual matters—and provides guidance, warnings, and prescriptions for ritual action. The Ifá corpus contains 256 ''Odù'' (chapters), each containing verses (''ẹsẹ̀'') that convey myths, proverbs, and prescriptions. The oral tradition of Ifá is vast, requiring years of study to master.

In 2005, [[UNESCO]] designated the Ifá divination system as a [[Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity]].

===Ancestor veneration===
{{Main|Ancestor veneration in Ayorin}}

Veneration of ancestors (''Ẹ̀gún'') is a fundamental aspect of Ìbẹmìílò practice. The ancestors are considered the closest spiritual allies of the living—they have experienced human existence and can intercede on behalf of their descendants. Every household maintains an ancestral shrine where offerings of food, water, and [[kola nut]]s are made, and where family members communicate with their forebears.

The annual '''Ẹ̀gún Festival''' (''Odún Ẹ̀gún'') honors the collective ancestors with elaborate masquerades, music, and feasting. Masked performers called '''Ẹ̀gúngún''' embody the spirits of the ancestors, dancing through the streets and blessing the community. These festivals reinforce family and community bonds while affirming the continued presence and relevance of the ancestors in daily life.

===Sacrifice and offerings===

Sacrifice (''ẹbọ'') is a central practice in Ìbẹmìílò, serving as a means of communication with the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' and ancestors. Offerings range from simple libations of water, palm oil, or [[palm wine]] to more elaborate sacrifices of animals (chickens, goats, rams) or, historically in rare cases, larger offerings. The purpose of sacrifice is not to appease angry gods but to establish and maintain reciprocal relationships: humans offer what they have, and the ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀'' respond with blessings, protection, and guidance.

The ethical dimension of sacrifice is emphasized: offerings must be given freely and with proper intention, and the meat of animal sacrifices is typically shared among the community, ensuring that no one goes hungry.

===Shrines and temples===

Ìbẹmìílò worship takes place at various types of sacred spaces:

* '''Household shrines''' (''ìbọ̀kún''): Small altars in the home dedicated to family ancestors and personal ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀''.

* '''Community shrines''' (''ìbọ̀dè''): Larger shrines serving a neighborhood or village, often dedicated to a major ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀''.

* '''Temples''' (''ìlè''): Major religious complexes, often associated with important pilgrimage sites or historically significant locations. The most famous is the [[Temple of Ọbàtálá at Ifẹ̀]], considered the spiritual heartland of the Ayori people.

* '''Natural sacred sites''': Rivers, forests, mountains, and trees believed to be dwelling places of ''Ìmọ̀lẹ̀''. The [[Ọ̀ṣun River]] and [[Sacred Grove of Ọ̀ṣun]] at [[Oṣogbo]] are among the most important.

